@{ ViewData["Title"] = "How to read a prescription"; }
<article style="max-width:70ch;">
<h1>How to read your prescription</h1>
<p class="muted">Every optician writes the same numbers, even if the layout differs.</p>

<h2>OD and OS</h2>
<p>OD is your right eye and OS is your left. They are Latin abbreviations used on
every prescription in the world. Some prescriptions write R and L instead.</p>

<h2>SPH — sphere</h2>
<p>The main strength of the lens, in dioptres. A minus number corrects short
sight, a plus number corrects long sight. It always moves in steps of 0.25.</p>

<h2>CYL and AXIS — cylinder and axis</h2>
<p>These two go together and correct astigmatism. The cylinder is the strength;
the axis, from 1 to 180 degrees, is the angle it sits at. One is meaningless
without the other, which is why our form refuses a cylinder with no axis.</p>

<h2>ADD — reading addition</h2>
<p>Extra magnifying power for close work, used for bifocal and progressive
lenses. If your prescription has an ADD value, choose a lens type that supports
it when you order.</p>

<h2>PD — pupillary distance</h2>
<p>The distance in millimetres between the centres of your pupils. It positions
the optical centre of each lens in front of your eye. Most people do not know
theirs — <a href="/guides/pd">our try-on tool measures it for you</a>.</p>

<h2>Prism and base</h2>
<p>Less common. If your prescription has a prism value it must also state a base
direction: in, out, up or down.</p>

<div class="alert alert-info">
<strong>Not sure?</strong> Upload a photo of the paper prescription instead of
typing it in, and one of our opticians will read it for you.
</div>
</article>
